  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An area of rain will attempt to move into the region \ntomorrow into tomorrow night as isentropic lift occurs on a warm \nfront. Uncertainty still exists on how far the northern extent will \nmake it, with the CAMs generally split. Some members like the HRRR \nfavor a surface low beginning to develop and bring the rain up into \ncentral and northern Vermont, while others like the NAM keep it more \nof an open wave and restrict precipitation to southern Vermont. \nTherefore, a period of rain is likely in southern Vermont tomorrow \nafternoon and evening while it is more of a chance for rain farther \nnorth. The rain will be stratiform and light, with expected amounts \nup to around a quarter inch in southern Vermont. Surface high \npressure builds down from Canada Friday into Friday night, bringing \na much cooler and drier airmass. By Friday night, temperatures \nshould fall into the 40s and 30s, with patchy frost possible in the \ncolder hollows of the Adirondacks and Northeast Kingdom. Highs will \nbe cooler on Saturday with temperatures topping out in the 60s with \nlow humidity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Following a washed out cold front by the end of the work \nweek, trends have continued to move towards the ECMWF solution \nkeeping most, if not all, precipitation south of the area Saturday \nnight. The baroclinic zone responsible for the area of potential \nshowers will be draped more over central and southern New York with \nbetter forcing southwest of our region. That said, model solutions \nSunday into Monday denote a subtle redevelopment of a surface low in \nsouthern New England which could bring some light shower chances to \nmainly southern Vermont. Ensemble variations denote less than 40% of \nmembers with rain over our region Saturday-Sunday. Our flow pattern \nwill remain confluent through the weekend, so any precipitation that \ndoes materialize will likely be short lived. Model solutions begin \nto vary by early next week in terms of precipitation chances. An \nupper level high over the Great Lakes will set up a dry corridor \nfrom the Great Lakes into northern New England, while a moisture \naxis over the Mid Atlantic attempts to push north. Model uncertainty \nin how much dry air there is will determine if moisture and \nassociated shower potential are able to penetrate northward into our \nregion, or if dry subsident air keeps our shower chances shunted to \nthe south. Trends have shifted the high a bit east, more centered \nover our north early next week, which bodes some better confidence \nin a drier start to next week. Regardless, zonal northwesterly flow \nwill keep our temperatures through the weekend into early next week \non the seasonably cool side with highs fairly consistent in the mid \nto upper 60s, and lows in the low to mid 40s, with some cold hollows \nhovering about the upper 30s. A sign of the changing seasons, our \nupper air pattern in the ensembles is beginning to show hints of \nsome CAD by Tuesday, as the high to our north shifts further east, \nwhich would lead to some cool trapped air east of the Greens by mid \nnext week. Sky conditions favor mainly cloudy conditions through the \nextended.",
